q1. check adjacency dgit difference.

problem: given string s number check the absolute diff which should be atmost 2 means 0 , 1, 2 is allowed... if consecutive digits have difference of atmost true then return true else return false

🔎 What You Initially Thought
Tried to see if two‑pointer or sliding window applies.

Got confused because string ≠ array of strings.

Considered using a flag variable and breaking on violation.

Thought sliding window might help (like maintaining sums), but realized it gives wrong results.

👉 Mistake: Overcomplicating with sliding window/two‑pointer when the problem is local adjacency only.

✅ Correct Way to Think
Pattern recognition:

Only neighbors matter → no need for sliding window.

Two‑pointer is unnecessary because we’re not comparing ranges.

Just a single linear scan.

Algorithm:

Loop from i=1 to n-1.

Compute abs(s[i] - s[i-1]).

If any > 2 → return false.

Else return true.

Complexity:

Time: O(n).

Space: O(1).

Code

class Solution {
public:
    bool isAdjacentDiffAtMostTwo(string s) {
        for (int i = 1; i < s.size(); i++) {
            int diff = abs((s[i] - '0') - (s[i-1] - '0'));
            if (diff > 2) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
};
📊 Dry Run Example
s = "132"

|1-3| = 2 → ok

|3-2| = 1 → ok
✅ return true

s = "129"

|1-2| = 1 → ok

|2-9| = 7 → violation → return false

🧩 Memory Hook
👉 “Neighbor gap ≤ 2 → safe string.”  
That’s the quick recall line.

🚀 Interview Checklist
Start with brute force idea: “Check each pair.”

Explain why sliding window/two‑pointer isn’t needed: adjacency only.

Show linear scan solution.

Mention complexity.

Edge cases: string length 1 (always true), very large string (still O(n)).

❌ Mistakes to Avoid
Don’t force sliding window/two‑pointer when only neighbors matter.

Don’t forget to convert char → int (s[i] - '0').

Don’t miss edge cases like single‑digit strings.

Don’t skip explaining why you rejected other patterns — interviewers want to see reasoning.


