q4. smallest unqiue subarray

🧩 Problem Essence
You’re given an array nums.

Task: find the shortest subarray that contains all distinct elements of the entire array.

Output: length of that subarray.

Example:

nums = [3,3,3] → distinct set = {3}.

To cover all distinct elements, you need the whole array → answer = 3.

nums = [1,2,2,3] → distinct set = {1,2,3}.

Shortest subarray covering {1,2,3} is [2,3,1] or [1,2,3] → length = 3.

🔎 What You Initially Thought
You mixed it up with “smallest window with unique elements” (no duplicates).

That gave wrong answers like 1 for [3,3,3].

Mistake: You were checking uniqueness inside the window, instead of covering all distinct elements of the array.

✅ Correct Way to Think
Step 1: Find how many distinct elements are in the array (target = set.size()).

Step 2: Use sliding window + hashmap:

Expand right pointer until window contains all distinct elements.

Then shrink from left to minimize window while still covering all distinct elements.

Update answer with window length.

Step 3: Return the minimum length found.

⚡ Contest‑Style Code
cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int smallestUniqueSubarray(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        unordered_map<int,int> freq;
        unordered_set<int> distinct(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        int target = distinct.size();   // total unique elements

        int l = 0, ans = n+1, cnt_uni = 0;
        for(int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
            freq[nums[r]]++;
            if(freq[nums[r]] == 1) cnt_uni++; // new unique element added

            while(cnt_uni == target) {
                ans = min(ans, r - l + 1);
                freq[nums[l]]--;
                if(freq[nums[l]] == 0) cnt_uni--; // unique element removed
                l++;
            }
        }
        return (ans == n+1 ? -1 : ans);
    }
};
📊 Dry Run Example
nums = [1,2,2,3]

Distinct = {1,2,3}, target=3.

Expand until window covers all 3 → at right=3, window=[1,2,2,3].

Shrink left → [2,2,3] still covers {2,3}, but missing 1 → stop.

Minimum window length = 3. ✅

🧩 Memory Hook
👉 “Cover all distinct → shrink to minimum.”

🚀 Interview Checklist
Start with brute force: “Try all subarrays, check if they contain all distinct elements.”

Spot inefficiency: “Too slow, O(n²).”

Optimize: “Use sliding window + hashmap to maintain counts dynamically.”

Dry run [3,3,3] and [1,2,2,3] to prove correctness.

Mention complexity: O(n) time, O(n) space.

❌ Mistakes to Avoid
Don’t confuse with “no duplicates” problem.

Don’t reset counters incorrectly (like cnt_uni=0).

Don’t forget edge cases: all identical elements, single element array.

Don’t jump straight to optimized solution — walk interviewer through brute → observation → optimized.
