q3. largest local values in a matrix 2

🧩 Problem Essence
You’re given a matrix (grid) of integers.

For each non‑zero cell with value x:

Look at all cells within x steps in both row and column directions.

Skip the four “corner” cells exactly at (i±x, j±x).

If none of those considered neighbors are greater than x, then this cell is a local maximum.

Task: count how many such local maximum cells exist.

🔎 What You Initially Thought
You first tried to connect it with sliding window/hashmap (like in subarray problems).

Got confused because here it’s 2D adjacency, not 1D ranges.

Mistake: Overcomplicating with wrong patterns.

Correct realization: It’s just matrix traversal + neighbor checks.

✅ Correct Way to Think
Brute force baseline:

For each cell, check all neighbors within distance x.

If any neighbor > x, mark false.

Else count++.

Observation:

Only need to check within bounds.

Skip corners explicitly.

Complexity:

Each cell checks at most (2x+1)² neighbors.

With constraints (≤200×200), brute force is fine.

⚡ Contest‑Style Code
cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int largestLocalValues(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {
        int n = grid.size(), m = grid[0].size();
        int ans = 0;

        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for(int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
                int x = grid[i][j];
                if(x == 0) continue;

                bool isLocalMax = true;
                for(int r = i - x; r <= i + x; r++) {
                    for(int c = j - x; c <= j + x; c++) {
                        if(r < 0 || r >= n || c < 0 || c >= m) continue;
                        if(abs(r - i) == x && abs(c - j) == x) continue;
                        if(grid[r][c] > x) {
                            isLocalMax = false;
                            break;
                        }
                    }
                    if(!isLocalMax) break;
                }
                if(isLocalMax) ans++;
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
📊 Dry Run Example
Matrix:

Code
0 0 0
0 2 0
0 0 0
Cell (1,1)=2 → neighbors within distance 2 are all 0 → no one bigger → count++.

Others are 0 → skipped.
👉 Answer = 1.

🧩 Memory Hook
👉 “Check neighbors within x, skip corners, count kings.”

🚀 Interview Checklist
Start with brute force idea: “Check each cell and its neighbors.”

Explain why sliding window/hashmap isn’t needed: adjacency only.

Show brute force solution.

Mention complexity and constraints.

Dry run one example to prove correctness.

❌ Mistakes to Avoid
Don’t confuse with 1D subarray problems.

Don’t forget to skip corners.

Don’t miss edge cases: all zeros, single cell, uniform matrix.

Don’t jump straight to optimized solution — walk interviewer through brute → observation → final.
